# content-admin — empty env templates (mirrors the `env:` list in module.manifest).
# The generator appends these (blank) to the project's .env.example section; set
# real values in .env and NEVER commit them.
#
# REQUIRED before the CMS will run correctly on first boot:
#   CMS_KEY / CMS_SECRET   two distinct random strings (e.g. `openssl rand -hex 32`).
#                          KEY identifies the instance; SECRET signs access tokens.
#                          Keep them stable — changing SECRET invalidates sessions.
#   CMS_ADMIN_EMAIL        the first admin account, auto-created on first boot when
#   CMS_ADMIN_PASSWORD     the database is empty (use a strong password).
#   CMS_DB_PASSWORD        password for the bundled, internal-only CMS PostgreSQL.
#
# OPTIONAL:
#   CMS_PUBLIC_URL         Directus PUBLIC_URL. Leave blank to default to "/"
#                          (relative URLs — correct for the internal-only setup,
#                          including reaching /admin over an SSH tunnel). Set it to
#                          a full https URL only if you later expose the CMS.
#
# NOTE: these are GLOBAL names (the contract appends module env verbatim, without a
# per-project prefix). If you run content-admin in more than one project and want
# each CMS to have its own credentials, override the relevant `${CMS_*}` values in
# that project's services/<name>/<name>.content-admin.compose.yml.
CMS_KEY=
CMS_SECRET=
CMS_ADMIN_EMAIL=
CMS_ADMIN_PASSWORD=
CMS_DB_PASSWORD=
CMS_PUBLIC_URL=
